Cross-Platform Migration of SYBASE ASE from AIX to Liux:
Step-by-Step Migration (XPDL Method)
1. Preparation on AIX (Source)
  Quiesce the Database: Ensure no transactions and application connections are active to maintain data integrity.
  Single-User Mode: Set the database to single-user mode: sp_dboption <db_name>, 'single user', true.
  Flush and Checkpoint: Run sp_flushstats and then checkpoint to ensure all pages are written to disk.  
    Note: sp_flushstats is used to manually move table and index statistics from in-memory storage to the permanent system tables, systabstats and sysstatistics  
          sp_configure "sysstatistics flush interval"
  Perform dbcc checkstorage and dump:
    dbcc checkstorage (database_name) - Fast, High-level physical/allocation check. It verifies page allocation, Object Allocation Map (OAM) entries, pointer consistency, and text column chains.
    Generate the full database dump file: dump database <db_name> to '<path_to_dump>'. 
    BCP out of login and roles as backup to restore user permissions, copy interface file, configuration file and runserver file.

2.  Setup on Linux (Target)
    OS requirements: 
      Sybase login for ownership, Allocate shared memory required like previou or more.
      SAP strongly recommends disabling Transparent Huge Pages (THP) on Linux, as they can cause latency spikes and performance degradation in database workloads.
      Check for latest linux patch requied as per sybase documentation of particular version.
      Configure Environment: Adjust shared memory settings (e.g., kernel.shmmax) in /etc/sysctl.conf and install required 32-bit libraries if using 64-bit Linux.
        Request for shared memory for sybase server minus the required by OS standard.
        Check using ipcs -m , free g and sp_configure 'max memory', 'value multiple of 2k', sp_configure 'allocate max shared memory'
        Create required filesystems for data, log and tempdbs
      As SYBASE user install ASE binaries: Install the same version (or higher) of ASE on the Linux server and setup sybase environment.
      Create Server and configure: Recreate the necessary database devices (data and log) and an empty database of at least the same size as the source.
      Restore interface file and configuration file or atleast compare and set it to same on new configuration file and start server.
     
3. Execution and Conversion
  Load Database: Transfer the dump file to Linux and execute: load database <db_name> from '<path_to_dump>' and set the database options.
  Restore users and permission from previous using BCP IN.
  Run sp_post_xpload: After the load, you must run sp_post_xpload to rebuild indexes and system objects that were affected by the endian change.
    sp_post_xpload can take an extremely long time because it processes indexes sequentially.
    Increase Parallel Degree: Set the 'max utility parallel degree' to its maximum (255) to allow ASE to consume all available resources for index rebuilding, 
                                                                        again can be increased to a value equal to sp_configure 'number of worker processes'.
    Expand Sort Buffers: Increase the 'number of sort buffers' to speed up the index creation process. sp_configure 'number of sort buffers'
    Procedure Cache: Boost the procedure cache size. Large index sorts require substantial procedure cache to avoid paging.
    Large I/O: Configure the cache with 16K or 32K I/O pools to speed up sequential scans during the index rebuild process.
    Asynchronous I/O: Ensure asynchronous I/O is enabled at the OS level on Linux to reduce wait times during heavy write operations.
      [sybase@mongopri install]$ cat /proc/sys/fs/aio-max-nr
      65536
      [sybase@mongopri install]$ cat /proc/sys/fs/aio-nr
      1025
      sp_configure "disk i/o structures",65536
  Online Database: Bring the database online: online database <db_name>. 

4. Post-Migration Validation
  Ensure you run the specific 16.0 scripts after the conversion to stabilize the system:
  Recompile Procedures: Use dbcc upgrade_object to recompile all stored procedures, triggers, and views from their source text in syscomments.
  Update Statistics: Immediately follow the migration with update statistics on all tables to ensure the query optimizer has accurate data for the new platform.
